# 250x250 matrix multiplication over λ□ primitive 63-bit ints: ../matmul, one
# literal apart (ExampleMatMul.matMulProgramOf 250).
#
# This program exists to be TIMED. Its point is that it is the same term as
# `matmul` -- same declarations, same shape, same generated Java, so the same
# gen/build cost -- with only the four `(tPrim (primInt N))` size literals
# changed. 250^3 is 7.1x the arithmetic of 130^3, which lifts the run stage out
# of the couple of seconds where JVM startup dominates and makes a change in the
# generated code's runtime visible. Anything that moves here and not in `matmul`
# is runtime, not compile time.
#
# Arend-authored, exactly like ../matmul: prog.ast is `ExamplePrint:matMulSexpr250`
# and the two attribute files are `matMulAttrsCText`/`matMulAttrsOCamlText`, all
# regenerated by test/tools/regen-arend-asts.sh. The attribute files are the four
# primitive-op declarations and are independent of the matrix size, so they are
# byte-identical to ../matmul's.
#
# 250^3 = 15625000, still far below 2^62, so C/OCaml int63 and Java's `long`
# agree here as they do at 130 -- which is why all three backends are declared:
# the differential check keeps a timed run honest about correctness too.
#
# Why 250: measured java_run is 3.4 s at 130, 8.6 s at 200, 21.3 s at 250 and
# 92.5 s at 300 -- the last is more than 2x off the cubic trend, i.e. at 300 the
# program is largely measuring heap and GC behaviour. 250 is the largest size
# still on the trend line. Any other size is one literal away; see
# ../../../benchmarks.md and tools/regen-arend-asts.sh.
#
# NOT the program to run while working -- ../matmul is (it is the fast one).
expected=15625000
backends=java ocaml c
runtime=int63
xfail=
note=250^3; ../matmul one literal apart, sized so the run stage is worth timing
